---

The catalog format selected in `Settings` > `Catalogs` > `Format` sets which
measurement form a new specimen uses. A single project can still hold specimens
from more than one taxon group.

| Catalog format | Taxon group | Measurement page |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Mammalogy` | Mammals | [Mammals](/en/usages/specimens/measurements/mammals/) |
| `Ornithology` | Birds | [Birds](/en/usages/specimens/measurements/birds/) |
| `Herpetology` (beta) | Herpetofauna | [Herpetofauna](/en/usages/specimens/measurements/herpetofauna/) |
| `Invertebrate zoology` (beta) | Invertebrates | [Invertebrates](/en/usages/specimens/measurements/invertebrates/) |
| `Ichthyology` (beta) | Fishes | [Fishes](/en/usages/specimens/measurements/fishes/) |
| `Paleontology` (in development, not in current releases) | Fossils | [Paleontology](/en/usages/specimens/measurements/paleontology/) |

Mammal catalogs can also show bat fields; see
[Mammals](/en/usages/specimens/measurements/mammals/#bat-fields).

## Shared fields

These fields behave the same way across measurement forms:

- **Weight units**: weight fields support `g`, `kg`, and `lbs`, defaulting to
  `g`. Changing the unit updates only the label and does not convert the entered
  number. In a fish lot, changing the lot's unit updates its individuals' unit
  as well, and `Lot summary` converts individual weights to the lot's unit.
- **`Sex`**: restricted controlled vocabulary shared by every taxon group.
  `Male`, `Female`, and `Unknown` are always available. To enable
  `Gynandromorph`, `Hermaphrodite`, `Female?`, or `Male?` (a question mark
  records examiner uncertainty), open `Settings` > `Specimens` and select `+`
  next to the option. An added option can be removed only when no specimen uses
  it.
- **`Life stage`**: editable controlled vocabulary shared by all released taxon
  groups, replacing older numeric age fields. Defaults are `Egg`, `Larva`,
  `Nymph`, `Pupa`, `Neonate`, `Juvenile`, `Subadult`, `Adult`, `Metamorph`, and
  `Unknown`. Configure choices in `Settings` > `Specimens` > `Life Stages`.
